Subject: [PATCH v10 0/6] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the mail relay jail
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev

Agents need to send and read p7x.dev mail without holding the
Fastmail credentials. This series builds lab-mail, the relay jail
that holds them, and points lab-claude's mail clients at it.

Patch 1 is the spec. Patches 2 to 5 build lab-mail one service at a
time: the jail and its credential mount, smtprelay for sending,
mbsync for the append-only copy of the lists folder, and Dovecot to
serve that copy read-only over IMAP. Patch 6 sets up msmtp, mbsync
and a separate notmuch profile in lab-claude, so the lab-devel list
keeps its jj maildir.

Testing. None of the services has run. I cannot build or start a
jail from here, so the acceptance test in the spec is still to do on
beastie. What I checked here, as fact:
- remotes.env from patch 3, sourced by sh(1) after /etc/rc.subr and
  under set -a as rc.subr does, with jq 1.8.2 from lab-claude, turns
  a mail-creds of "me@p7x.dev" and "p@ss:w/rd #x y%" on two lines
  into
  smtps://me%40p7x.dev:p%40ss%3Aw%2Frd%20%23x%20y%25@smtp.fastmail.com:465
  and leaves no _login or _password in the environment.
- The same remotes.env exits 1 through rc.subr's err, with
  "ERROR: ... needs the login on line 1 and the password on line 2",
  for a missing file, an empty file, one line only, an empty first
  line, and an empty second line. A second line with no trailing
  newline is accepted.
- Each dist.mtree lists exactly the files under dist/, and the shell
  files pass sh -n.

Inference, not tested:
- smtprelay decodes the percent-escapes. It parses its remote with
  Go's net/url, whose Userinfo returns decoded values.
- A jail started before the operator creates /jails/data/lab-mail/mail
  fails to mount it, and jail(8) names the missing path.
- jq in lab-mail is the same jq as in lab-claude. I have not looked
  inside lab-mail.

rc.subr sources remotes.env for every smtprelay command, not only
start, so with a broken mail-creds "service smtprelay stop" and
status fail too, as fact from reading /etc/rc.subr. Fixing the file
fixes them. I left it unguarded to keep remotes.env simple; checking
rc_arg would limit the check to start, if you prefer.

The mbsync side does not check the lines itself. With a missing line
mbsync gets an empty login or password and Fastmail refuses it, so
the failure shows in the fetch log (inference, not tested).

Changes in v10:
- mail-creds holds the login on line 1 and the password on line 2,
  so nothing depends on where a space falls in the line. The spec,
  the README and its printf example, remotes.env and mbsyncrc all
  follow, and both readers take each line whole with sed -n 1p and
  sed -n 2p. Pat asked for this on patch 3.
- remotes.env refuses to start smtprelay unless both lines are
  present and not empty, as Pat asked on patch 3. The patch 3
  message says why.
